<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sena dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th–13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sena dynasty ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> from the late 11th century to the early 13th century, after taking over from the Pala Empire. Its kings, notably Vijaya Sena and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ballal-sena">Ballala Sena</a>, patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanism">Brahmanical Hinduism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> literature, and the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayadeva">Jayadeva</a> is associated with the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmana-sena">Lakshmana Sena</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhtiyar-khalji">Bakhtiyar Khalji</a>'s surprise attack on Nadia around 1204 drove Lakshmana Sena east, and the dynasty survived briefly in eastern Bengal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sena-dynasty.jpg" alt="Sena dynasty" width="320" height="296" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Map created from DEMIS Mapserver, which are public domain. Koba-chan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Senas.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Sena dynasty was a Hindu dynasty that succeeded the Pala Empire and ruled much of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. Its rulers traced their origin to the south. The Deopara Prashasti describes the founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanta-sena">Samanta Sena</a>, as a migrant Brahmaksatriya from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, a term suggesting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> who took up arms and became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriyas</a>. According to the historian P. N. Chopra the Sena kings were probably Baidyas. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plate suggests the Senas settled in western Bengal before Samanta Sena's birth and entered Pala service as sāmantas in Rāḍha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanta-sena">Samanta Sena</a> came Hemanta Sena, who usurped power and styled himself king in 1095. His successor Vijaya Sena (r. 1096–1159) laid the foundations of the dynasty in a reign of over sixty years, and by its end its territory included Vaṅga and part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varendra">Varendra</a>. The Palas were ousted entirely sometime after 1165. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ballal-sena">Ballala Sena</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Gaur</a> from the Pala, ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> delta and made Nadia a capital as well. He married Ramadevi, a princess of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire</a>. Lakshmana Sena succeeded him in 1179 and expanded the dynasty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, possibly up to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>.</p><p>In 1203–1204 Muhammad Bin Bakhtiyar Khalji, a general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghurid-empire">Ghurid Empire</a>, captured Nadia, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmana-sena">Lakshmana Sena</a> retreated to the south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. His sons Vishvarupa Sena and Keshava Sena kept their independence in southern and eastern Bengal, probably until at least 1230, and the Tabaqat-i-Nasiri suggests that his descendants ruled there until at least 1245 or 1260.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Senas built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples and monasteries, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ballal-sena">Ballala Sena</a> is credited with the Dhakeshwari Temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a>. They patronised literature, and Govardhana, Sarana, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayadeva">Jayadeva</a>, Umapati and Dhoyi were poets at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmana-sena">Lakshmana Sena</a>. Later genealogies say Ballala Sena introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulinism">Kulinism</a>, which conferred titles of nobility on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanas">Brahmanas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayastha">Kayasthas</a> and Vaidyas, but most scholars regard those accounts as unhistorical. Later dynasties in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, Suket and Mandi claimed descent from the Senas.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sena dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It arose in the early 13th century in eastern Bengal, around Vikrampur and the later Suvarnagrama (Sonargaon), as <b>Sena</b> power collapsed in western Bengal after Bakhtiyar Khalji's conquest of Nadia.</blockquote><a href="/state/deva-dynasty-of-bengal">Deva dynasty of Bengal</a></li><li><blockquote>Muhammad Bakhtiyar Khalji, a Turkic-Afghan commander serving the Ghurids, raided Bihar and then, around 1204, surprised the <b>Sena</b> king Lakshmana Sena at Nadia.</blockquote><a href="/state/khalji-dynasty-of-bengal">Khalji dynasty of Bengal</a></li></ul></section>
